ae844370b06d82ce892e052ecd1c114519a6bb31e153e3bcb10c2c4806d56.svg)\n\nIn our cases, for $x\\in(-\\pi,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166573 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166573 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=-6x-2$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x).$ \n\n![](images/4f/4f5284fb6fed9d16cafa85a546e65db1b9ebf5a5211d870b24dfe87bb1ccb49b.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166573 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43229a59-0b01-55ff-8b10-b1238360d277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166573 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-6x-2$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi (-6x-2) \\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-6x-2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(-6x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{6}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-6\\pi -2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{6}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0-\\frac{6}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n,$ we have\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim (-2-3\\pi) + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}} \n\\cdot \n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\cos(nx)}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166574 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166574 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for $a_0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166574 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c8b4459b-e0ed-56cc-ab06-5be09baa7363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166574 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3\\pi }(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }(\\sqrt{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166957 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186167286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186315072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166957 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 175921