 #uuid "bffb8473-8281-5879-a88a-b0d8ca02dcb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167285 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167285 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340794" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167285 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167285 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in (-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167285 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fcf50127-6a15-5bfa-b137-f8e9738bae25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167285 #edb/ref 1074 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}F(x), & x\\in [0,\\pi ), \\\\ F(-x), & x\\in (-\\pi ,0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, for $x\\in (-\\pi ,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{2}-2(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167288 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167288 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167288 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167288 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167288 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=2x+3$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ Select the correct missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$ below.\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(3+\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot {{field-2}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167288 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2a82489-ea8c-529e-9b98-bcb721239fc4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167288 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2x+3$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2x+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+3\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(2x+3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }-\\frac{2}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((2\\pi +3)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\frac{2}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0+\\frac{2}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-1)}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )=(-1)^{n},$ we have\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x)∼(3+\\pi )+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4((-1)^{n}-1)}{\\pi {n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167289 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186314028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167289 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340814" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167289 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167289 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=3{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for ${a}_{0}.$\n\n${a}_{0}=$ {{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167289 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d95b499-c990-5e2e-96ad-0c45f0c2fce8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167289 #edb/ref 1074 "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+2$ for $x\\in (0,\\pi ).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(3{x}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[{x}^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }({\\pi }^{3}+2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =2({\\pi }^{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167934 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167934 #edb/ref 1061 595703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167934 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "70e9a79d-82de-580b-aef4-7b85b63184e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167934 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167935 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167935 #edb/ref 1061 595704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167935 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8aa6cef0-eda2-592b-9aca-4965f3055e89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167935 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167936 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167936 #edb/ref 1061 595707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167936 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8896eaaa-38ce-50d6-9371-a6e16a56ffbe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167936 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167937 #edb/ref 1061 595710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167937 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f1fe41d9-fcaa-5738-8ea0-e820f8f39957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167937 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168044 #edb/ref 1064 "In the earlier topics, we saw that *symmetry* can force many Fourier coefficients to be zero automatically.\nIn particular, if a $2\\pi$-periodic function is *even*, then its Fourier series has *cosine terms only*.\n\nA **Fourier cosine series** uses this idea when a function is initially given only on the half-interval $[0,\\pi),$ but we want a full Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ (and then a $2\\pi$-periodic extension to all real $x$).\n\nTo do this, we start with a function $F(x)$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and define its **even extension** $f(x)$ to $(-\\pi,\\pi)$ by\n![](images/e2/e2e07048ac4750968947e45443f59096f395b159d4f7a846410a711194b11a14.svg)\n\nFor example, a function $F(x),$ defined on $x \\in [0,1),$ and its even extension $f(x)$ to $x \\in (-1,1)$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/33/33d92f280a5672c8d9d94553ccc1ada2a9c5ea1ff12344ca8c9aeca9f44c40c6.png)\n\nBy construction, $f$ is an *even* function, meaning $f(-x)=f(x)$. Because $f$ is even, its Fourier series on $[-\\pi,\\pi)$ has *cosine terms only*:\n\n$$\nf(x)\\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} a_n\\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is even and $\\cos(nx)$ is even for any $n\\ge 1,$ the product $f(x)\\cos(nx)$ is even too. Therefore, the cosine coefficients can be computed using the *half-range formulas*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for $n\\ge 1,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo in practice, the workflow is the following:\n\n1\\. Start with $F$ on $[0,\\pi)$ and form the even extension $f$ on $(-\\pi,\\pi).$\n\n2\\. Compute $a_0$ and $a_n$ from the half-range integrals on $[0,\\pi).$\n\n3\\. Write the Fourier cosine series of $f.$\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168044 #edb/ref 1065 14676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e9cc2bdd-3ebe-5